      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path:</strong> F6 at 65% captures another Iranian kinetic strike within 72h; with F3 and F4 both at 5%, no near-term diplomatic or economic circuit-breaker is available to interrupt the exchange cycle.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path:</strong> Both sides absorb tactical attrition without triggering a broader front or third-party intervention; the tit-for-tat cadence continues at current intensity for weeks.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path:</strong> F3 at 5% and F4 at 5% together assign only a marginal probability to diplomatic resumption or Hormuz normalisation before their respective horizons; this path requires an event not yet in the data.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN (descriptive, ~200 words) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · ~200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iran's state media — Tasnim, IRNA, Mehr, IRIB — framed CENTCOM's destruction of the five tankers on September 8 as "maritime piracy" and "economic terrorism against the Iranian people." The framing strategy emphasised the ships' civilian character and omitted discussion of the IRGC's prior attempts to shoot down a US warship that CENTCOM cited as the trigger for the operation. Tasnim — editorially linked to IRGC — asserted the destroyed vessels "carried humanitarian fuel" without documentary support. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>The IRGC's missile salvo against Al Azraq on September 8 was presented in state media as a "targeted and legitimate act of precision retaliation." Jordan's report that 18 of 20 missiles were intercepted and that the remaining two fell in desert terrain was not communicated through official channels; Mehr News stated only that "targets were struck." → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>The IAEA's Iran-critical resolution of September 9 was characterised by IRNA as a construct of "the Zionist lobby within the agency" and dismissed as illegitimate. The IRGC's public statement designating the Hormuz blockade as a "strategic and deterrent weapon" in Iran's possession circulated widely in state media networks on September 9–10 — a rhetorical posture that holds the diplomatic channel formally open while excluding any concession. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>
      <p>Araghchi's statement barring negotiation resumption until the US compensates for MOU violations was carried by IRNA as a "principled and legally correct position" rather than a diplomatic impasse. The framing preserves a formal bridge back to the MOU negotiating framework — Iran does not explicitly close the channel but conditions its use on US acts not presently on offer. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

